    "body": "Charts, without bundling a charting library into Hazel.\n\nReplaces the d3-backed chart projector in #2350 (which this supersedes \u2014 that\napproach put the whole chart vocabulary inside the implementation, so every new\nchart kind, scale, or annotation was an OCaml change *plus* a JS change, and\n`ChartProj` could not be tested at all because its output only existed inside\nd3's DOM).\n\nHere there is no `Chart` type and no chart projector. A chart is an ordinary\n`HTML` value built from this branch's `Node` and `Create` escape hatches (which\nrender SVG), and the charting vocabulary \u2014 scales, nice-domain rounding, tick\nselection, axes, bands, arcs, legends, palette \u2014 is Hazel source in\n`hazel-programs/charts/charts.hz` that a reader can fork. Adding a stacked bar\nor a log scale is a change to Hazel code. No d3, no npm additions.\n\n### What the implementation gains instead\n\nNot chart knowledge \u2014 a way to draw *values*:\n\n- **`HtmlRenderer`**, a rich probe (`^^probe_html`), renders a probe sample as\n  whatever its value describes: static HTML as DOM, or an\n  `(init, update, view, subs)` 4-tuple as a running app keyed on the probe's id.\n  This is what a computed chart needs \u2014 `Chart.bar(sales)` is an application, so\n  its HTML only exists after evaluation. It is the value-side counterpart to\n  `HTMLProj`, and mirrors its two commit modes.\n\n  When the probed expression is *itself* html, a handler commits by **rewriting\n  the source** to `f(H)` and letting the program's own pipeline evaluate it \u2014\n  splicing the handler by the name the evaluator recorded on it, so the edit\n  lands on the definition and every call sees it. Evaluating the transform inside\n  the renderer cannot work: a probe has no elaborated syntax and no environment\n  to resolve `f` in.\n\n- ~~**`View.docked`** lets a projector send a secondary surface to the Projectors\n  panel while keeping its inline presence \u2014 distinct from\n  `ProjectorCore.Placement`, which moves the whole projector and leaves a chip.\n  `ProbeProj` owns rich-probe placement, so every renderer docks with no\n  per-renderer support, `TableRenderer` included.~~\n\n  Dropped. `hazel-html` had meanwhile answered the same question \u2014 where an open\n  rich probe draws \u2014 with `drawer_mode`, `auto_rich`/`rich_off` and a `below`\n  slot, opening the drawer when content exceeds `inline_rows_cap`. Two designs\n  for one concern, so this branch took upstream's: `renderer_placement`,\n  `ToggleRendererPlacement`, the `docked` slot, `ProjectorView.docked_views` and\n  the panel's docked-probe pass are all gone. Nothing else needed changing \u2014\n  `renderer_placement` had no consumers outside `ProbeProj`.\n\n  What is *not* a competing design is the chart work itself: `HtmlRenderer` stays\n  registered beside upstream's `CardRenderer`, and gained the two members\n  `hazel-html` added to the renderer signature \u2014 `auto_applies` (HTML and MVU\n  apps are real evidence, so `true`) and `drawer_rows` (arbitrary-height UI, so\n  above `inline_rows_cap` and drawer-bound).\n\n- **`project_html`** (Nut menu \u2192 HTML) renders an HTML-valued result in the\n  evaluation output, mirroring the existing Tables toggle and off by default.\n  Gated on the constructor's *type* rather than its name, and projects only the\n  outermost node \u2014 every child of an element is itself an HTML constructor\n  application, so projecting each one would wrap a projector around every element\n  in the tree.\n\n### Slides\n\nFour, under **Charts**:\n\n| Slide | What it shows |\n|---|---|\n| Charts | The `Svg`/`Scale`/`Chart` library plus a gallery of all five kinds |\n| Linked Views | Drag a bar; a pie derived inside `view` follows |\n| Table Explorer | One row-polymorphic app over two B2T2 tables |\n| Steps | A probe inside a recursion \u2014 one sample per step \u2014 and html that edits its own source |\n\nTable Explorer reuses B2T2's `Student` / `GradebookEntry` tables and the\nidiomatic `groupByRetentive` from `table-api-utilities-groupbyretentive.hz`, so\nthe app is driven by the real table API. Steps is the pair of things a probe can\ndo that a projector cannot: record *many* samples, and stay additive so the\nsource it draws is still on screen and still typeable.\n\n### Also here\n\n- **`27577e6122` fixes a bug on this branch** (see the review comments on\n  `Ascriptions.re`): annotating a parameter with a builtin recursive alias\n  (`HTML`, `JSON`, `Cmd`, `Sub`) made `case` on that argument silently stop\n  reducing. `compact_builtin_recs` leaves those aliases compact (`Var(\"HTML\")`)\n  and `Typ.unroll` doesn't resolve a `Var`, so the two constructor guards never\n  matched and the ascription never pushed in. Bisected to `bce50ac5fc`; widened\n  to `JSON` by the dev merge `b18b27d8bd`. **That commit is cherry-pickable on\n  its own** if you'd rather take it directly.\n- `simple_icon` hoisted into `SvgUtil`; `Icons` and `TableRenderer` each had a\n  copy, since haz3lcore can't depend on web.\n- `HTMLProj.init` now sees through `Parens`, which auto-display needs in order to\n  get the single piece a projector replaces.\n- `docs/charts.md` covers the three-layer split, the projector-vs-probe\n  distinction, and the traps \u2014 including why evaluate-and-splice is the wrong\n  way to edit source from a probe.\n\n### Tests\n\n`Test_Charts` asserts on the value tree the library produces, including that\ngrouped series align by label rather than position (the fix from #2350's review),\nthe degenerate cases, and the scale/tick arithmetic. Plus `Test_HtmlRenderer`,\n`ExpToSegment.ProjectHtml` (one projector at the root, not one per element), and\ntwo regression tests for the `Ascriptions` fix in `Test_Evaluator_Sum_Types`.\n\nBased on `hazel-html`, so it needs #2115 to land first. Merged with `hazel-html`\nat ~~`33e0053fb8`~~ `f21ac5f8dd`.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
